              "type": "text",
              "text": "Goldbach’s conjecture states that ‘every even number greater than 2 is the sum of two primes’. Show that this is the case for even numbers from 32 to 62. (A conjecture is a mathematical statement that is thought to be true but for which no proof is known.)",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "Sample answers:\n\n$32=3+29$, $34=3+31$, $36=5+31$, $38=7+31$, $40=11+29$, $42=11+31$, $44=13+31$, $46=17+29$, $48=11+37$, $50=7+43$, $52=11+41$, $54=13+41$, $56=13+43$, $58=5+53$, $60=7+53$, $62=43+19$.",
      "worked": "One pair of primes for each even number is:\n\n$$\\begin{align*}32&=3+29\\\\34&=3+31\\\\36&=5+31\\\\38&=7+31\\\\40&=11+29\\\\42&=11+31\\\\44&=13+31\\\\46&=17+29\\\\48&=11+37\\\\50&=7+43\\\\52&=11+41\\\\54&=13+41\\\\56&=13+43\\\\58&=5+53\\\\60&=7+53\\\\62&=43+19\\end{align*}$$\n\nEach addend has exactly two factors, 1 and itself, so each is prime. These sums cover every even number from 32 to 62.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
